\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

We study how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance in tabletop manipulation. Given a pretrained VLA with vision encoders $\phi_v$ and a language-conditioned action decoder $\pi$, standard fine-tuning minimizes the action prediction loss $\mathcal{L}_\text{action} = \mathcal{L}(\pi(\phi_v(o_t), l), a_t^*)$, where $o_t$ is the visual observation, $l$ is the language instruction, and $a_t^*$ is the demonstration action. We augment this objective with an auxiliary spatial prediction head $h$ that maps intermediate visual features to a spatial target $s_t^*$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda(t) \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}(h(\phi_v(o_t)), s_t^*)
\label{eq:total_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda(t)$ is a time-varying weight. The spatial target is either a BEV occupancy grid or an egocentric depth map. The auxiliary head is discarded at inference and adds no cost at deployment.

We use OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} % Kim et al., "OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model", arXiv:2406.09246, 2024
with the OFT fine-tuning recipe~\cite{kim2025oft} % Kim et al., "Fine-Tuning Vision-Language-Action Models: Optimizing Speed and Success", arXiv:2502.19645, 2025
as the base model. OpenVLA's vision backbone concatenates patch features from a DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} % Oquab et al., "DINOv2: Learning Robust Visual Features without Supervision", TMLR 2024, arXiv:2304.07193
encoder (dimension 1024) and a SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} % Zhai et al., "Sigmoid Loss for Language Image Pre-Training", ICCV 2023, arXiv:2303.15343
encoder (dimension 1152) to form a 2176-dimensional representation per spatial token. The auxiliary heads receive these concatenated features, so their gradients flow through both the spatial (DINOv2) and semantic (SigLIP) encoders simultaneously. We apply LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} % Hu et al., "LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models", ICLR 2022, arXiv:2106.09685
with rank 32, $\alpha = 16$, and no dropout, yielding a conservative effective scaling factor of $\alpha / r = 0.5$.

\subsection{BEV Occupancy Head}
\label{sec:occ_head}

The occupancy head predicts a $64 \times 64$ binary bird's-eye-view occupancy grid from the cached patch features $\mathbf{F} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times N \times 2176}$. The architecture has four stages:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A linear projection from 2176 to 256 dimensions, reshaped to $\mathbb{R}^{B \times 256 \times 16 \times 16}$.
    \item A transposed convolution ($256 \to 128$ channels, kernel size 4, stride 2) with batch normalization and GELU activation, producing $32 \times 32$ spatial resolution.
    \item A second transposed convolution ($128 \to 64$ channels, kernel size 4, stride 2) with batch normalization and GELU, producing $64 \times 64$ resolution.
    \item A $1 \times 1$ convolution ($64 \to 1$ channel) with sigmoid activation, yielding $\hat{y} \in [0, 1]^{B \times 1 \times 64 \times 64}$.
\end{enumerate}
The head contains 1.2M parameters, or 0.016\% of the full 7.65B-parameter model.

Ground-truth occupancy maps are generated offline by replaying recorded demonstration trajectories in MuJoCo and rendering top-down binary grids at each timestep. Each cell is set to 1 if an object body intersects the corresponding workspace region. We train with focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal} % Lin et al., "Focal Loss for Dense Object Detection", ICCV 2017, arXiv:1708.02002
to handle the class imbalance between occupied and free cells:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{occ} = \text{FocalLoss}(\hat{y}, y^*;\; \alpha = 0.25,\; \gamma = 2.0)
\end{equation}
The auxiliary loss weight follows an exponential decay schedule:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_\text{occ}(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp\!\left(-\frac{t}{50000}\right)
\label{eq:occ_decay}
\end{equation}
This schedule progressively reduces the occupancy gradient's contribution so that action prediction dominates in later training stages. The decay can be interpreted as a curriculum: the model first develops spatial structure through the auxiliary signal, then refines motor behavior under the primary objective.

\subsection{Monocular Depth Head}
\label{sec:depth_head}

The depth head shares the occupancy head's architecture with two modifications: the final activation is ReLU (to enforce non-negative depth values) instead of sigmoid, and the training loss is smooth L1:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{depth} = \text{SmoothL1}(\hat{d}, d^*)
\end{equation}
with the same exponential decay schedule ($\lambda_\text{depth}(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t / 50000)$). The depth head has approximately 1.2M parameters.

Ground-truth depth maps are rendered from the egocentric camera viewpoint in MuJoCo at $64 \times 64$ resolution using state replay. Unlike the BEV occupancy grid, which provides an allocentric top-down layout of the workspace, the depth map encodes distances from the robot's camera to scene surfaces. This egocentric frame aligns with the robot's visual observation and directly encodes proximity information relevant to collision avoidance: nearby surfaces produce small depth values, providing a natural gradient for spatial awareness. The BEV frame, by contrast, represents object layout in a coordinate frame orthogonal to the camera view, requiring the encoder to learn a non-trivial viewpoint transformation.

\subsection{Ablation Design}
\label{sec:ablation}

We evaluate five training conditions designed to disentangle the mechanisms by which auxiliary spatial supervision affects collision avoidance.

\textbf{Vanilla.} Standard LoRA fine-tuning with action loss only. Any collision avoidance behavior emerges solely from imitating demonstration trajectories.

\textbf{Full OCC.} LoRA fine-tuning with the BEV occupancy head receiving ground-truth labels. Gradients from $\mathcal{L}_\text{occ}$ flow through both the DINOv2 and SigLIP encoders. This condition tests whether BEV spatial supervision improves collision avoidance.

\textbf{Random OCC.} Identical to Full OCC, but the occupancy labels are replaced with random binary grids that contain no spatial information. Gradient magnitudes and computational costs match Full OCC. Comparing Random OCC to Full OCC isolates the contribution of spatial content: if both perform equally, gradient flow regularization is the dominant mechanism; if Full OCC is superior, the BEV spatial signal provides additional benefit beyond regularization.

\textbf{Detach OCC.} Identical to Full OCC, but gradients are detached at the boundary between the vision encoders and the occupancy head. Only the head's 1.2M parameters receive gradient updates from $\mathcal{L}_\text{occ}$; the encoders see only $\mathcal{L}_\text{action}$. This condition tests whether gradient flow through the encoder is a necessary condition for the auxiliary task's safety effect. If Detach performs worse than Vanilla, the disconnected head acts as a gradient sink that consumes optimization capacity without providing regularization.

\textbf{Depth.} LoRA fine-tuning with the monocular depth head receiving ground-truth egocentric depth maps. This condition compares allocentric (BEV occupancy) and egocentric (camera-frame depth) spatial reference frames while holding the auxiliary training mechanism constant.

We additionally evaluate a curriculum variant (\textbf{OCC-Cutoff}) that applies occupancy supervision for the first 35{,}000 steps then disables it, testing whether late-stage auxiliary gradients interfere with action prediction refinement. This condition separates the effect of early-stage spatial regularization from potential late-stage gradient interference.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation design. Each pairwise comparison isolates one factor in the interaction between auxiliary supervision and collision avoidance. The interpretation of the Depth vs.\ Random OCC comparison depends on whether random labels match or underperform real spatial labels (see \S5).}
\label{tab:ablation_design}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Comparison & Isolated Factor \\
\midrule
Full OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Effect of auxiliary spatial supervision \\
Random OCC vs.\ Full OCC & Spatial content vs.\ gradient flow \\
Detach OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Necessity of encoder gradient flow \\
Depth vs.\ Full OCC & Egocentric vs.\ allocentric frame \\
Depth vs.\ Random OCC & Effect of spatial signal fidelity \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This design systematically varies three factors: the presence of gradient flow to the vision encoders, the informational content of the auxiliary labels, and the spatial reference frame. Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design} summarizes the pairwise comparisons and what each isolates.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Benchmark and Tasks}

We evaluate on the SafeLIBERO benchmark~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, which augments the LIBERO-Spatial suite with collision-aware evaluation. SafeLIBERO comprises 32 scenarios across 10 tabletop manipulation tasks organized into two difficulty levels: Level~I (basic spatial reasoning) and Level~II (complex multi-step manipulation). Each task requires the agent to reach, grasp, and place objects among surrounding obstacles. Several tasks deliberately position objects in close proximity (e.g., a bowl resting on a ramekin), creating natural collision challenges that test whether the agent can complete the instructed task without disturbing nearby objects.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}

We measure two complementary metrics aligned with the VLSA-AEGIS protocol~\cite{hu2025vlsa}. \textbf{Task Success Rate (TSR)} is the fraction of episodes in which the robot completes the instructed task. \textbf{Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)} is the fraction of episodes with zero unintended object displacement, computed as $\text{CAR} = 1 - n_{\text{collide}} / N$, where $n_{\text{collide}}$ counts episodes whose maximum L1 \texttt{body\_xpos} displacement exceeds a threshold of $0.001$. For each of the 10 tasks, we run 20 rollouts per random seed across 3 seeds (42, 43, 44), yielding 60 rollouts per task and 600 total. We report mean and 95\% confidence intervals, with paired $t$-tests and Cohen's $d$ effect sizes for pairwise comparisons.

\subsection{Experimental Conditions}

We design five conditions that form a mechanism ablation chain, progressively isolating the role of occupancy-grounded gradient flow:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Vanilla}: OpenVLA-OFT fine-tuned without any auxiliary supervision (baseline).
  \item \textbf{+Occ}: BEV occupancy auxiliary head with gradients flowing into the vision encoder (\texttt{occ\_detach=False}).
  \item \textbf{+Occ-Detach}: Occupancy head with detached gradients (\texttt{occ\_detach=True}), testing whether gradient flow to the encoder is necessary.
  \item \textbf{+Depth}: Depth prediction auxiliary head (same architecture, labels from Depth Anything V2), testing whether collision avoidance is specific to occupancy or arises from any geometric auxiliary task.
  \item \textbf{+Random-Occ}: Occupancy head trained with randomized labels, controlling for the regularization effect of an auxiliary loss.
\end{itemize}

\noindent The chain Vanilla $\to$ +Random-Occ $\to$ +Depth $\to$ +Occ-Detach $\to$ +Occ isolates, in turn, auxiliary regularization, geometric supervision, and occupancy-specific gradient flow.

\subsection{Implementation Details}

All conditions share the same base model: OpenVLA-OFT 7B~\cite{kim2025oft}, pre-trained on Open X-Embodiment. We fine-tune with LoRA (rank 32, $\alpha{=}16$) for 50K steps using AdamW (weight decay $0.01$), a batch size of 8, a constant learning rate of $5 \times 10^{-4}$, and 3 random seeds per condition. The LoRA adapter adds 110.8M trainable parameters (1.45\% of 7.65B total).

The occupancy head (1.2M parameters) is a convolutional decoder that maps concatenated DINOv2+SigLIP patch features (2176 dimensions) to a $64{\times}64$ BEV occupancy grid. The first 256 patch tokens from the primary camera view are selected and projected via a linear layer ($2176{\to}256$), then reshaped into a $16{\times}16$ spatial map. Two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) with BatchNorm and GELU upsample this map to $64{\times}64$, and a $1{\times}1$ convolution produces the binary occupancy logits. The head is trained with Focal Loss ($\alpha{=}0.25$, $\gamma{=}2.0$); the occupancy loss weight $\lambda_{\text{occ}}$ starts at $0.01$ and decays exponentially as $\lambda_{\text{occ}}(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t/\tau)$ with $\tau{=}50$K steps. The depth head uses the same decoder architecture with continuous regression targets. Each run trains in approximately 12.9 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (98\,GB). Evaluation uses \texttt{MUJOCO\_GL=egl} for headless rendering with deterministic rollout seeds.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Why Does Gradient Flow Improve Collision Avoidance?}
\label{sec:why_gradient}

The ablation results reveal a consistent ordering: conditions with auxiliary gradient flow through the vision encoder (Random OCC at 0.806 CAR, Full OCC at 0.797) outperform Vanilla (0.780), while Detach OCC (0.745) falls below all others. Three candidate mechanisms can explain this pattern.

\textbf{Hypothesis 1: Anti-overfitting regularization.}
Auxiliary gradients prevent the vision encoder from overfitting to the action prediction objective, preserving more general spatial features in the DINOv2 and SigLIP representations. Multi-task learning theory predicts this effect: shared representations trained on multiple objectives retain broader feature diversity than those specialized to a single task~\cite{caruana1997multitask, ruder2017overview}. Lippl and Lindsey~\cite{lippl2024implicit} show that multi-task training in overparameterized networks induces $\ell_{1,2}$ mixed-norm regularization that encourages feature sharing, while single-task fine-tuning exhibits nested feature selection that discards unused pretrained features. Under this hypothesis, Random OCC and Full OCC both regularize because both supply non-trivial gradients through the encoder---the label content matters less than the gradient signal itself. The observation that Random OCC (0.806) matches or exceeds Full OCC (0.797) is consistent with this account, though the difference is within seed variance (2--3 seeds per condition) and should not be over-interpreted.

\textbf{Hypothesis 2: Optimization landscape smoothing.}
The auxiliary loss modifies the curvature of the total loss landscape, steering LoRA adapter updates away from sharp minima of the action loss alone. Sharp minima generalize poorly under distribution shift~\cite{foret2021sam}, and SafeLIBERO evaluation introduces a distributional shift (obstacles absent during training, present at test time). If the auxiliary loss flattens the loss surface, the resulting adapter weights may produce policies that degrade more gracefully when the scene layout differs from training. This hypothesis predicts that \emph{any} auxiliary loss with comparable gradient magnitude should help, which aligns with Random OCC's strong performance. A direct test would compare the Hessian spectra of Vanilla and auxiliary-trained checkpoints; we leave this analysis to future work.

\textbf{Hypothesis 3: Gradient diversity.}
When fine-tuning with action loss alone, encoder gradients are dominated by a single objective, which may cause gradient alignment across batches and reduce the effective rank of weight updates. An auxiliary task provides an independent gradient direction that maintains update diversity. Unlike Hypothesis~1, which focuses on feature preservation, this hypothesis emphasizes the optimization dynamics: auxiliary gradients break correlations in the update trajectory rather than preserving specific features. The distinction is testable by measuring gradient cosine similarity between the action and auxiliary heads across training steps.

All three hypotheses predict that gradient flow is necessary (Detach should underperform) and that label content is secondary to gradient presence (Random should match Full OCC). The current data cannot distinguish between them---doing so requires gradient-level diagnostics (Hypothesis~3), loss landscape analysis (Hypothesis~2), or representational probing (Hypothesis~1). We present all three to guide future mechanistic investigation.

\subsection{Why Is Detach Harmful?}
\label{sec:why_detach}

Detach OCC produces the lowest CAR (0.745) among all conditions, falling 3.5 percentage points below Vanilla (0.780). This result may appear surprising: the Detach condition has identical architecture and forward computation to Full OCC, differing only in gradient routing.

The primary mechanism is a forward-pass interference without compensating gradient signal. The auxiliary head contains BatchNorm2d layers that consume encoder feature statistics during the forward pass, modifying the batch-level statistics that the encoder representations are conditioned on. In the Full OCC and Random OCC conditions, the auxiliary loss sends gradients back through the encoder that allow it to adapt to these statistics---effectively co-optimizing the shared representations. With \texttt{stop\_gradient}, the encoder receives gradients from $\mathcal{L}_\text{action}$ alone and cannot adapt to the statistical perturbation introduced by the auxiliary head's forward computation. The encoder bears the cost of the auxiliary pathway (altered batch statistics, additional forward computation) without receiving the auxiliary-task training benefit that gradient flow provides. The 3.5pp deficit relative to Vanilla, observed across 2 seeds, suggests that gradient flow is not merely helpful but is a necessary condition for the auxiliary task to avoid harming performance.

\subsection{Implications for VLA Safety}
\label{sec:implications}

\textbf{Practical recommendation.} The finding that Random OCC matches Full OCC in CAR carries a direct practical implication: practitioners fine-tuning VLAs for safety-sensitive deployment can attach an auxiliary head---even one with uninformative labels---with gradient flow to the vision encoder and expect an auxiliary-task training benefit for collision avoidance. This eliminates the need for ground-truth spatial labels, which require simulator access or depth sensors, and reduces the auxiliary task to a generic training-time mechanism with zero inference overhead. Our experiments with random and structured occupancy labels suggest this pattern, though testing additional auxiliary tasks (e.g., contrastive objectives, reconstruction losses) would strengthen this recommendation.

\textbf{Complementarity with runtime safety.} Our approach modifies the training signal to produce policies that collide less frequently, but provides no formal safety guarantee for any individual episode. Runtime constraint methods such as CBF-based action filtering~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf} offer per-step guarantees but add inference cost and require access to obstacle geometry. These two strategies are complementary: auxiliary-task training reduces the frequency of unsafe action proposals, while runtime filtering can catch the remaining violations. A natural extension is to construct a barrier function that operates on the spatial features already learned by the occupancy-trained encoder, avoiding the need for explicit geometric representations at test time.

\textbf{Zero inference cost.} Unlike existing VLA safety methods that add modules at inference~\cite{hu2025vlsa, zhang2025safevla, zhai2026cofreevla}, auxiliary spatial supervision modifies only the training phase. The auxiliary head is discarded after training, so the deployed model has identical latency, memory footprint, and computational cost to the Vanilla baseline.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{Single architecture.} All experiments use OpenVLA-OFT with LoRA rank 32. Whether the gradient-flow regularization effect transfers to other VLA architectures (e.g., RT-2~\cite{brohan2023rt2}), other adapter configurations, or full fine-tuning remains untested.

\textbf{Single benchmark.} We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level~I, which tests 10 tabletop manipulation tasks with static obstacles. The results may not generalize to dynamic obstacles, multi-step tasks (Level~II), or real-world deployment where sim-to-real transfer introduces additional distributional shift.

\textbf{Effect size.} The overall CAR improvement is 1.7--2.6 percentage points over Vanilla. While this is consistent across conditions with gradient flow, the magnitude is modest. Per-task analysis shows larger effects on physically constrained tasks where the baseline CAR is low, but these are also the tasks with the highest seed variance.

\textbf{Seed sensitivity.} Auxiliary conditions exhibit higher cross-seed variance than Vanilla (standard deviation $\sim$0.026 vs.\ $\sim$0.012 for overall CAR, a 2.2$\times$ ratio). The Full OCC condition shows the largest single-seed range (6.2pp across 3 seeds), indicating that auxiliary supervision amplifies sensitivity to initialization. With 2--3 seeds per condition, confidence intervals overlap for several pairwise comparisons. The ordering Random OCC $>$ Full OCC $>$ Vanilla is directionally consistent but not statistically confirmed at conventional significance levels. The Random vs.\ Full OCC comparison is based on 2 seeds vs.\ 3 seeds respectively---this asymmetric sampling means the directional conclusion requires confirmation from a third Random OCC seed. Scaling to 5+ seeds per condition would clarify whether the Random--Full OCC gap reflects a real difference or sampling noise.

% [PENDING DEPTH RESULTS] — Section below to be filled when depth experiment data is available.
\subsection{Depth vs.\ BEV Occupancy}
\label{sec:depth_discussion}

This section will compare egocentric depth supervision against allocentric BEV occupancy supervision, analyzing whether the spatial reference frame affects the regularization mechanism and collision avoidance outcomes. Pending completion of depth condition evaluation at 50,000 steps across all seeds.

