\section{Introduction}

Vision-language-action (VLA) models map visual observations and language instructions directly to robot actions, achieving high task success rates on manipulation benchmarks~\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla, kim2025oft}. On SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a 10-task tabletop benchmark with static obstacles in the workspace, a fine-tuned OpenVLA model completes 90.7\% of tasks but achieves only 78.0\% collision avoidance rate (CAR), both measured over 3 seeds and 50 rollouts per task. On the three most physically constrained tasks, collision rates reach 53--93\%. One task yields 100\% task success with only 32.5\% collision avoidance---the robot completes the goal while colliding in two-thirds of episodes. Task success does not imply behavioral safety.

Auxiliary spatial prediction tasks---occupancy grids, depth maps, point clouds---have been widely adopted to improve VLA manipulation performance~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, wu2025spatialvla}. These methods inject 3D structure into vision encoders pretrained on 2D images, consistently improving success rates and generalization. Separately, VLA safety methods apply runtime action filtering via control barrier functions~\cite{hu2025vlsa} or constrained reinforcement learning~\cite{zhang2025safevla} to reduce collisions. These two research threads remain disconnected: no spatial VLA method quantitatively evaluates collision avoidance, and no VLA safety method exploits spatial representation learning. Whether auxiliary spatial supervision affects collision avoidance---and whether any such effect derives from the spatial information itself or from training dynamics of the auxiliary objective---is an open question.

We present the first factorial study isolating how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance. We train OpenVLA-OFT~\cite{kim2024openvla, kim2025oft} under five conditions that systematically vary gradient flow and label content: Vanilla (action loss only), Full OCC (BEV occupancy with ground-truth labels), Random OCC (random binary labels with matched gradient flow), Detach OCC (ground-truth labels with encoder gradients detached), and Depth (egocentric depth prediction) [PENDING DEPTH RESULTS]. We find that auxiliary gradient flow through the vision encoder is a primary factor behind collision avoidance improvement, while label content plays a secondary role. Conditions with gradient flow---Random OCC (CAR 0.806, 2 seeds) and Full OCC (0.797, 3 seeds)---outperform Vanilla (0.780, 3 seeds), while Detach OCC (0.745, 2 seeds), which blocks encoder gradients, falls 3.5 percentage points below Vanilla. That random labels perform comparably to real occupancy labels (difference of 0.9pp, directionally consistent across seeds but preliminary at current sample sizes) suggests that gradient flow regularization, rather than spatial information, is the primary driver. Our contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Mechanism identification.} We provide evidence that auxiliary gradient flow is a primary factor behind collision avoidance improvements in VLA fine-tuning. Gradient flow is necessary: removing it (Detach OCC) degrades CAR by 3.5pp below the no-auxiliary Vanilla baseline (0.745 vs.\ 0.780, 2 and 3 seeds respectively). Spatial label content is secondary: random labels achieve comparable CAR to ground-truth occupancy labels (0.806 vs.\ 0.797, preliminary at 2--3 seeds per condition).

    \item \textbf{Factorial ablation design.} We introduce a controlled multi-condition study that isolates gradient flow, spatial content, and spatial reference frame as factors in the interaction between auxiliary supervision and collision avoidance. Each pairwise comparison answers one mechanistic question (Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design}), providing a reusable methodology for analyzing auxiliary task effects in VLA training.

    \item \textbf{Practical guideline.} Attaching an auxiliary prediction head to the vision encoder during fine-tuning---even one trained on uninformative labels---improves collision avoidance, provided gradients flow through the encoder. No ground-truth spatial labels are required, and the auxiliary head is discarded after training, adding zero inference cost.
\end{enumerate}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa} (10 tasks drawn from LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}) using OpenVLA-OFT with LoRA fine-tuning. Across conditions with gradient flow, CAR improves by 1.7--2.6pp over Vanilla; blocking gradient flow reduces CAR by 3.5pp. Section~2 reviews related work on VLA safety, spatial representations, and multi-task regularization. Section~3 describes the auxiliary head architectures and ablation design. Section~4 presents experimental results and mechanism analysis. Section~5 discusses implications, limitations, and connections to multi-task learning theory.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We investigated how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance. A factorial study on OpenVLA-OFT across five training conditions revealed that auxiliary gradient flow through the vision encoder is a primary factor behind collision avoidance improvement, while the spatial content of auxiliary labels plays a secondary role. Conditions with gradient flow---Random OCC and Full OCC---improved collision avoidance rate by 1.7--2.6 percentage points over the Vanilla baseline (CAR 0.806 and 0.797 vs.\ 0.780), whereas Detach OCC, which blocks encoder gradients, fell 3.5pp below Vanilla (0.745). That random binary labels matched ground-truth occupancy labels in CAR (preliminary at 2--3 seeds per condition) points to gradient-based regularization rather than spatial information as the operative mechanism. [PENDING DEPTH RESULTS]

These results yield three contributions. First, we identify auxiliary gradient flow as a primary factor in collision avoidance improvement and show that blocking it (Detach OCC) actively harms performance relative to no auxiliary task at all. Second, our factorial ablation design---isolating gradient flow, spatial content, and spatial reference frame as independent factors---provides a reusable methodology for analyzing auxiliary task effects in VLA training. Third, we establish a practical guideline: attaching an auxiliary prediction head with gradient flow to the vision encoder during fine-tuning improves collision avoidance at zero inference cost, without requiring ground-truth spatial labels.

Several directions remain open. First, testing additional auxiliary task types---contrastive objectives, reconstruction losses---would establish whether gradient-flow regularization generalizes beyond occupancy prediction. Second, distinguishing among the three candidate mechanisms proposed in Section~\ref{sec:why_gradient} (anti-overfitting regularization, optimization landscape smoothing, gradient diversity) requires gradient-level diagnostics: gradient norm tracking across training, loss landscape curvature analysis via Hessian spectra, and representational probing of encoder features. Third, evaluating on other VLA architectures (e.g., RT-2), extended benchmarks (SafeLIBERO Level~II, dynamic obstacles), and real-world deployment would test the robustness of the gradient-flow effect beyond the single architecture and benchmark studied here.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\subsection{Benchmark and Tasks}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level I~\cite{hu2025vlsa} % Hu et al., "VLSA: Vision-Language-Action Models with Plug-and-Play Safety Constraint Layer", arXiv:2512.11891, 2025
, which places obstacles in close proximity to target objects across 10 tabletop manipulation tasks. Task difficulty varies with spatial layout: in simple configurations the obstacle is isolated from the manipulation path (e.g., an object placed far from the target), while in physically constrained scenarios the obstacle sits directly on or adjacent to the target (e.g., a bowl placed on top of a ramekin). Training data comes from LIBERO-Spatial~\cite{liu2023libero} % Liu et al., "LIBERO: Benchmarking Knowledge Transfer for Lifelong Robot Learning", NeurIPS 2023, arXiv:2306.03310
, comprising approximately 25,000 teleoperated demonstration episodes across the 10 tasks.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

We report two primary metrics. \textbf{Task Success Rate (TSR)} measures the fraction of evaluation episodes in which the robot completes the instructed manipulation. \textbf{Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)} measures the fraction of episodes with no collision, following the SafeLIBERO protocol: a collision is registered when the L1 displacement of the obstacle's body position (\texttt{body\_xpos}) exceeds a threshold of $10^{-3}$ during execution. We report both overall metrics (averaged across all 10 tasks) and per-task breakdowns, since ceiling effects on spatially simple tasks, where the baseline already achieves near-perfect CAR, compress overall differences and mask the effect on physically challenging tasks.

\subsection{Training Configuration}

The base model is OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} % Kim et al., "OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model", arXiv:2406.09246, 2024
with the OFT fine-tuning recipe~\cite{kim2025oft} % Kim et al., "Fine-Tuning Vision-Language-Action Models: Optimizing Speed and Success", arXiv:2502.19645, 2025
. LoRA adapters use rank 32, $\alpha = 16$, and no dropout. Each training sample consists of two input frames (\texttt{--num\_images\_in\_input 2}): the current observation and the preceding frame, providing temporal displacement cues. Image augmentation is enabled during training (\texttt{--image\_aug True}), applying random crops and color jitter. Training uses a batch size of 8, a constant learning rate of $5 \times 10^{-4}$, and runs for 50,005 steps with checkpoints saved every 10,000 steps. Each condition is trained with at least 3 random seeds (42, 43, 44); the depth condition uses 4 seeds (42, 43, 44, 45) for additional statistical power given that it is the primary comparison of interest. Evaluation uses 50 rollouts per task per checkpoint to reduce per-task confidence intervals to approximately $\pm$13 percentage points (compared to $\pm$21pp at 20 rollouts).

\subsection{Hardware and Compute}

Experiments run on servers equipped with 4 NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (98\,GB each). Each training run completes in approximately 12.9 hours at 1.08 iterations per second. The full experimental matrix (5 conditions $\times$ 3--4 seeds) requires approximately 200 GPU-hours for training and 50 GPU-hours for evaluation.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

We report mean and standard deviation across $N$ seeds per condition, with 50 rollouts per task (500 total episodes per seed). Given the limited seed counts (2--3 per condition), we report effect directions and magnitudes but note that formal significance requires additional replications. All results use the 50{,}000-step checkpoint unless noted otherwise.

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes overall TSR and CAR across conditions. Three patterns emerge.

\textbf{Gradient flow determines CAR ordering.} Conditions with auxiliary gradient flow through the vision encoder---Random OCC (CAR 0.806, 2 seeds) and Full OCC (0.797, 3 seeds)---both outperform Vanilla (0.780, 3 seeds). Detach OCC (0.745, 2 seeds), which blocks encoder gradients, falls 3.5 percentage points below Vanilla. This ordering---gradient-flow conditions above Vanilla, gradient-blocked condition below---is directionally consistent across all seeds.

\textbf{Label content is secondary.} Random OCC and Full OCC differ by 0.9 percentage points in CAR, a gap within cross-seed variance. Random binary labels---containing no spatial information---match structured occupancy labels in collision avoidance, consistent with gradient flow regularization as the primary factor rather than spatial content. This comparison is preliminary at 2 vs.\ 3 seeds.

\textbf{TSR remains stable.} TSR varies between 0.879 (Random OCC, 2 seeds) and 0.911 (Full OCC, 3 seeds) across conditions, with all values within one standard deviation of the Vanilla baseline (0.907). The auxiliary conditions affect collision avoidance without degrading task completion. Random OCC's lower TSR (0.879) is based on only 2 seeds with the highest cross-seed variance ($\sigma = 0.033$) and overlaps with Vanilla.

