\subsection{Mechanism Analysis}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four completed conditions form a $2 \times 2$ factorial structure crossing gradient flow (ON/OFF) with label content (structured/random or none). Table~\ref{tab:factorial} arranges the CAR values in this matrix.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Factorial decomposition of CAR by gradient flow and label content. Each cell shows the condition name and mean CAR. The column effect (gradient flow) dominates: both ON cells exceed their OFF counterparts.}
\label{tab:factorial}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
 & Gradient ON & Gradient OFF \\
\midrule
Structured labels    & Full OCC (0.797) & Detach OCC (0.745) \\
Random / no labels   & Random OCC (0.806) & Vanilla (0.780) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Gradient flow is the dominant factor.} The column effect---gradient ON minus gradient OFF---is $+$5.2 percentage points with structured labels (Full OCC vs.\ Detach OCC) and $+$2.6 percentage points with random or no labels (Random OCC vs.\ Vanilla). Averaging across rows, gradient flow accounts for a $+$3.9pp shift in CAR. Every gradient-flow seed exceeds the Vanilla mean; every Detach seed falls below it.

\textbf{Label content contributes marginally and inconsistently.} The row effect---structured minus random/none---is $-$0.9pp when gradient flows (Full OCC vs.\ Random OCC) and $-$3.5pp when it does not (Detach OCC vs.\ Vanilla). In neither case do structured labels outperform the random/no-label condition. The negative sign of the label effect in the gradient-ON row---random labels directionally outperforming real occupancy labels---runs counter to the expectation that spatial information would add value. At 2 vs.\ 3 seeds, this gap is too narrow to interpret reliably.

\textbf{Detach incurs a forward-pass cost without gradient compensation.} The asymmetry in gradient flow effects ($+$5.2pp with structured labels vs.\ $+$2.6pp with random/none) reflects the specific penalty of the Detach condition. Detach OCC and Vanilla both lack encoder gradients from the auxiliary loss, but Detach still runs the auxiliary head in the forward pass, introducing BatchNorm statistics that the encoder cannot adapt to without auxiliary gradients. This forward-pass interference without compensating gradient signal explains why Detach falls \emph{below} Vanilla rather than matching it.

Three candidate mechanisms---anti-overfitting regularization, loss landscape smoothing, and gradient diversity---can account for the gradient flow effect. All three predict that gradient flow is necessary and label content is secondary. Section~\ref{sec:why_gradient} develops these hypotheses; the current data cannot distinguish between them.


